  "source_slug2": "lost_documents_en",
  "source_article": "epc.txt, lost-documents.txt",
  "post_text": "The EPC fine in Spain starts at the advert, not the sale. 300 to 600 euros for marketing a property without one, before a single viewing happens.\n\nIf you publish listings, the sequence matters more than the rating.\n\nThe certificate must exist before the property is listed, and a copy goes into the notarial deed when it sells. Notaries may refuse to complete without it. So a missing EPC is not a completion problem, it is a marketing problem that surfaces on day one and again at the finish line.\n\nThe fines scale with the offence. Advertising without a valid certificate runs 300 to 600 euros. Failing to provide one at all, or falsifying it, runs 1,000 to 6,000. Each autonomous region manages its own inspections and penalties.\n\nThe fix is genuinely small. A certified technician inspects insulation, windows, heating, cooling and lighting, then issues the A to G rating. Budget 100 to 300 euros and less than a week, start to finish. The certificate lasts ten years and must be registered with the regional authority, which the technician will usually handle. A strong rating can also support the price and opens access to green funding and subsidies.\n\nIf the seller has lost theirs, the issuing technician or any licensed assessor replaces it for roughly 60 to 150 euros. No drama, just lead time.\n\nMy rule: no certificate on file, no listing goes live. Build it into your intake checklist.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #RealEstateSpain #EPC",
