  "source_slug": "nota_simple_en",
  "source_slug2": "land_registry_en",
  "source_article": "nota-simple.txt, land-registry.txt",
  "post_text": "The Nota Simple can quietly shrink your buyer's mortgage. If the cadastral value on it sits 10k under the price, the bank may lend 10k less.\n\nThat detail from the Land Registry catches foreign buyers off guard, and it is exactly the kind of thing an agent can flag early.\n\nThe document itself costs around 10 euros online and can arrive the same day. In person at the registry it is under 4 euros. You supply the property details or its identifier, the IDUFIR, which the seller or the agent usually has to hand. Anyone with a legitimate interest in buying can request one.\n\nWhat it shows: the registered owner and when they bought, the property description and square metres, boundaries, the land classification, and any charges. Mortgages, unpaid taxes, debts. In Spain those debts sit on the property, so your buyer inherits whatever that page reveals.\n\nTwo moments matter. Before the arras, because any debt or discrepancy is far easier to resolve before 10 percent changes hands. And at mortgage time, because when the registered cadastral value comes in below the offer, the bank looks at that lower figure, not the price your buyer agreed.\n\nIf the document shows something unexpected, the next call is a property lawyer, not the seller.\n\nOrder it for every serious buyer before the arras is drafted. Ten euros of paper beats six weeks of surprises.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#NotaSimple #BuyingInSpain #RealEstateSpain",
