  "source_slug": "arras_contract_en",
  "source_article": "property-contracts.txt",
  "post_text": "The arras draft arrives a couple of days before signing. That window exists so someone can request amendments. Most foreign buyers never use it.\n\nIf you sell to foreign buyers, this contract is where the deal is really made.\n\nWhat must be inside, checked line by line: an accurate description of the property, address, size and layout. The price. A completion date, usually two to three months out, and it has to leave enough time for the buyer's documentation and any mortgage. The penalties if either side pulls out. And the specifics: furniture the sellers leave, repairs they agreed to fund, whether appliances are included, whether water and electricity will be switched on. If it is not written here, it was never agreed.\n\nThe stakes are real on both sides. Once signed, a buyer who withdraws loses the 10 percent deposit. A seller who withdraws pays double. That is why the draft goes out a couple of days early, so the buyer or their representative can read it and push back.\n\nTwo more habits worth building. The 10 percent sometimes goes straight to the owners, which is common, but a safeguarded account at a financial institution is the safer route, so raise it. And on a new build, add completion date clauses of their own.\n\nSend the draft to your buyer the day it lands, with one line: read this now, not at the table.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #BuyingInSpain #RealEstateSpain",
