  "title": "An accepted offer binds nobody",
  "source_slug": "offer_accepted_en",
  "source_article": "offer-accepted.txt",
  "post_text": "In Spain an accepted offer is a gentleman's agreement. Either side can still walk away without penalty. What binds is the arras, and it can arrive within days.\n\nThat speed is the part foreign buyers never see coming.\n\nThe sequence: the property comes off the market, the buyer signs an offer contract, usually at the agency, and transfers 1,000 to 2,000 euros. That sum comes off the fees if the sale proceeds and is returned if it falls through. Nothing yet stops the seller changing their mind.\n\nThen the arras. Ten percent of the price, terms formally agreed, completion date fixed. Pull out as buyer and the deposit is gone. Pull out as seller and you repay double. It can be a matter of days between acceptance and this signature, which is a huge commitment on a short runway.\n\nBetween those two points sits the due diligence: the Nota Simple, confirming the registered owner, checking for debts tied to the property, verifying planning permissions and correct registration. And the money has to be ready to move through a Spanish account, because the transaction and everything after it runs through one.\n\nFrom deposit to keys usually takes six to ten weeks. The buyers who sail through are the ones whose NIE, account and funds existed before the offer did.\n\nSo ask the qualifying questions at the first viewing, not at the accepted offer.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #RealEstateSpain #BuyingInSpain",
