  "audience": "agents",
  "title": "The 3 percent withholding",
  "source_slug": "plusvalia_en",
  "source_article": "plusvalia.txt",
  "post_text": "When the seller is a non-resident, your buyer picks up two of the seller's tax jobs, each with its own deadline. Most discover this at the notary's table.\n\nIf you list homes owned by foreigners, this is worth explaining at the mandate, not the completion.\n\nJob one: the 3 percent withholding. The buyer holds back 3 percent of the purchase price and pays it to the tax authorities within one month of the sale. It is not an extra tax. It is a holding against the seller's capital gains bill, which runs 19 percent of the profit for EU and EEA residents and 24 percent for everyone else. If the final tax comes to less than the amount withheld, the seller is entitled to a rebate of the difference within the year.\n\nJob two: plusvalia, the municipal tax on the increase in urban land value. Normally the seller pays it, but when the seller is a non-resident, the buyer pays it instead, with 30 days from the transfer to get it to the local council before fines start. The amount depends on local rates and the years of ownership, up to a 20 year cap, and if the land value genuinely fell, there is an exemption.\n\nNone of this needs drama. It needs a written agreement before completion on who handles which payment.\n\nRaise it the moment you learn the seller lives abroad.\n\nGeneral information, not tax advice.\n\n#SpanishTax #SpainProperty #RealEstateSpain",
